WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.550
The David Pakman show@davidpakman.com we have
a hack day of youth today, so I'm particularly

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:04.799
glad that you're with me

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:05.990
and I'll get right into it.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.109
We have a major, major story to start with
today.

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:12.170
It's really one of the biggest stories of
the year, uh, in the world of social media

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.570
and online marketing.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:19.660
Uh, the issue of political ads and terms of
service has become a really huge issue and

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.640
it relates to politics so closely.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.410
And Twitter has decided to ban all political
advertising from its platform.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:33.880
Uh, the Twitter CEO Jack Dorsey says that
advertisers are getting an unfair advantage

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:40.140
in sending out misleading and inaccurate messages
and that Twitter is going to stop all political

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.560
ads, period.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.430
This was announced on Twitter and a multi
tweet thread by Jack Dorsey where he said,

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:51.030
quote, we've made the decision to stop all
political advertising on Twitter globally.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.710
We believe political message should reach
a political message.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.280
Reach should be earned, not bought.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.340
Of course, there are some claiming this is
a form of suppression of speech and it's not.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:08.230
And Jack Dorsey says this, and a subsequent
tweet in this thread, quote a final note,

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.400
this isn't about free expression.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.580
This is about paying for reach and paying
to increase the reach of political speech

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.890
has significant ramifications that today's
democratic infrastructure may not be prepared

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:22.890
to handle.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.680
It's worth stepping back in order to address.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.120
Now, interestingly, he says it first, they
thought maybe we'll ban ads just by political

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:37.920
candidates promoting a candidate in a particular
election, but then they correctly realize

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.160
that lots of what is going on, that that is
really political advertising is PACS that

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.500
may be supporting a candidate, which is sort
of like one level removed from the candidate.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:53.270
And then you're getting to issue based stuff
that is clearly political but wouldn't be

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.870
stopped if you simply say no ads for candidates.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.400
So they're banning all of it.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.270
Uh, although we have to figure out what the
implementation will be.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.840
So let's talk about that first.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.810
But then you know, I really want to talk about
the implications and the context in which

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.090
this is taking place.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:12.150
So implementation, this starts on November
22nd now.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.730
That's about three weeks from now.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:20.000
How will they decide if an ad is political
in nature because so many things are political

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.590
to some degree is an ad about keeping our
national parks clean from pro uh, pollution

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:31.730
and trash in the context of the climate crisis
is that political who gets to decide?

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.870
I don't know the answers to these questions.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:37.880
It's going to need to be sorted out and I'm
guessing that on some level Twitter will come

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:42.170
up with some rubric for determining what it
is that counts as political and there may

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:45.040
well be some disagreement about what that
is.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.030
The big thing here is that this is very clearly
a shot across the bow at Facebook and that

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:54.610
Mark Zuckerberg, you might remember and we
covered it, that Mark Zuckerberg testified

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.940
before the house last week.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.520
It went really poorly for him.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.690
I played the clips of Congresswoman Alexandria
Cossio Cortez asking him questions that he

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.420
wasn't even remotely able to answer in a satisfactory
way.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.430
Her mistake was interrupting his answers.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.390
I think that that is not the right look.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.700
You gain nothing by interrupting Mark Zuckerberg.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.490
If anything, you bail him out from fully being
able to expose that he doesn't know a lot

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:20.490
of these answers.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:27.810
That's my one critique of AOC in that, but
mostly it was Zuckerberg who was visibly confused,

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:34.000
not just about what Facebook policies should
be from a moral, ethical or legal standpoint,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.050
but even seemed confused about what Facebook
policies are.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.770
Uh, and what is clear is that Facebook and
Twitter are going to be taking drastically

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:45.950
different approaches to this issue of political
advertising and what is involved.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:51.550
Facebook is taking the ad money and they are
going to make some sort of little restrictions

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:58.070
around the edges to try to protect some semblance
of responsibility that they want to claim

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:02.090
to have about what they will allow a Mark
Zuckerberg talking about examples of, you

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.319
know, you can't target black voters.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.190
I think he said you can't target black voters
or black predominantly black zip codes with

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:12.260
an ad that misstates when election day is.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:16.230
That would be a form of voter suppression,
but the line between that and just a generally

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.380
dishonest ad is a blurry line.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.319
It became very clear that it was blurry.

00:04:20.319 --> 00:04:24.449
When Mark Zuckerberg testified Twitter is
doing something very different.

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:26.020
They're cutting this off at the pass.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.449
They're saying no political ads period.

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:32.620
Now Jack Dorsey doesn't mention Mark Zuckerberg
specifically in his tweets, but he does say

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.229
for example, that quote, we have witnessed
many social movements reach massive scale

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:39.620
without any political advertising.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:46.000
What he is responding to is Zuckerberg saying,
you know, if we tighten our policies on this

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:52.080
will disproportionately hurt grassroots groups
will disproportionately hurt challengers in

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.080
politics and we will be propping up the status
quo.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.689
That was Mark Zuckerberg, his argument, clearly
Jack Dorsey saying he doesn't agree with.

00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:02.559
I agree with that when he says we've seen
movements succeed even without paid political

00:05:02.559 --> 00:05:03.559
advertising.

00:05:03.559 --> 00:05:04.990
So I have a few thoughts on this.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:12.430
First of all, remember politicians, billionaire
tech CEOs, these are not heroic actors.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:13.740
We shouldn't idolize them.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.400
Jack Dorsey made a decision that well could
be the correct moral decision, but there is

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:24.490
no question that part of it was a calculation
based on the PR of it based on the timing

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.069
of last week's Facebook fiasco in front of
Congress.

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:33.119
So, number one, let's not focus our interpretation
on the morality of ethics or ethics of the

00:05:33.119 --> 00:05:34.369
decision.

00:05:34.369 --> 00:05:37.619
Even if on the merits it is the right decision
and it may well be.

00:05:37.619 --> 00:05:43.020
Secondly, I have to tell you, I know political
ads are big on Twitter.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:48.389
In my experience, the problem isn't so much
the political ads, which I see very few of

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:50.960
and can say, I don't want to see stuff like
this.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.830
I don't know if that's because of how I browse
Twitter.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:58.939
For me, the real problem, the real battle
lines are the terms of service and enforcement

00:05:58.939 --> 00:06:00.719
of those terms of service for users.

00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:03.369
For me, this has always been really the issue
on Twitter.

00:06:03.369 --> 00:06:06.970
I like the decision that was made about banning
political ads.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:09.759
That's a decision Twitter can make and they
can say, we're just not gonna have that on

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:10.759
our platform.

00:06:10.759 --> 00:06:15.180
Our platform will not allow a political issues
or candidates to buy influence.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.759
They're going to have to earn it.

00:06:16.759 --> 00:06:22.550
But what about high profile people who are
allowed to completely flout the terms of service?

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.800
For example, like Donald Trump himself.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.259
What about the totally soft way in which Twitter
has dealt with criticism about the political

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:33.599
nature of it's terms of service from both
left and right, sometimes rightly, sometimes

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:41.289
in a completely ridiculous way without adjudicating
right now as a um, uh, a particular terms

00:06:41.289 --> 00:06:45.909
of service decision as right or wrong Twitter
needs to deal with their terms of service

00:06:45.909 --> 00:06:48.759
enforcement in a serious and credible way.

00:06:48.759 --> 00:06:54.899
And I hope that they do, although this might
be a really PR friendly thing to do, banning

00:06:54.899 --> 00:06:59.789
political ads instead of dealing with the
mess of their terms of service and more specifically

00:06:59.789 --> 00:07:00.910
their enforcement.

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:05.659
One last thought on this, even if you ban
political ads in the formal sense where a

00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:12.129
candidate can run a paid campaign, uh, there
are still ways around this candidates or issue

00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:17.939
based organizations or packs, they can still
pay for retweets and tweets.

00:07:17.939 --> 00:07:23.180
There are a whole bunch of ways that you can
still effectively by reach on Twitter, even

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.689
if it is strictly speaking band.

00:07:25.689 --> 00:07:27.569
That is a bigger conversation and we'll see
if ultimately Twitter comments about that.

